| Concern | Detail |
|---|---|
| **`import.meta.hot` guard** | The entire HMR block is wrapped in `if (import.meta.hot)`. Vite statically replaces this with `false` in production, so the block is dead-code-eliminated. This is the Vite equivalent of webpack's `module.hot`. |
| **`import.meta.hot.data`** | Vite's persistence bag across swaps. Storing `teardown` here lets the *incoming* module call the *outgoing* module's cleanup before re-running setup — preventing double-registration of listeners, runaway timers, etc. |
| **`hot.accept(cb)`** | Called with no dependency array, so this module self-accepts. The callback receives the new module object; inside it we clean up the old instance and re-initialise. |
| **`hot.dispose(cb)`** | A safety net for the case where the module is removed entirely or the page unloads — ensures cleanup always runs. |
| **Production path** | The plain `else` branch calls `setup()` once with no overhead; in practice a bundler entry point might call `setup()` separately, but including it here keeps the module self-contained. |

