### How it works

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| **Initial load** | `setup()` runs at module evaluation time, starts a heartbeat interval, and stores the returned `dispose` function in the module-level `dispose` variable. |
| **File saved** | Vite detects the change and checks whether this module (or any ancestor) is a *self-accepting* HMR boundary. |
| **`import.meta.hot.dispose`** | Before Vite evaluates the new version it calls the registered dispose callback, which invokes `dispose()` and clears the interval—preventing duplicate timers or stale listeners. |
| **Module re-evaluation** | Vite executes the updated file from the top; `setup()` runs again, creating a fresh interval and a new `dispose` closure. |
| **`import.meta.hot.accept` callback** | Called with the new module object after evaluation; we log a confirmation. Because `setup()` already ran during evaluation, no further action is needed here. |
| **Production** | The entire `if (import.meta.hot)` block is dead code — Vite sets `import.meta.hot` to `undefined` during production builds and a minifier removes it entirely. |

